  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM MDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nThe subtropical ridge remains parked over the area, with very dry\nair aloft and at the surface. Surface dewpoints even at daybreak\ntoday were in the upper-30s over much of the lowlands, but they\nhave not mixed out much since then. We did see a modest increase\nin mid-level moisture, which bumped the PWAT up from 0.72 to 0.91\non the EPZ sounding between 00Z and 18Z. \n\nA few weak radar echoes are noted over Socorro County, where\ndewpoints in the 40s are more common. Cu buildups over the Black\nRange and Sacramentos are fairly flat based on webcams. \n\nThe forecast going forward is largely unchanged, with minimal\nprecip chances today, and just a slight tick up tomorrow in the\nhigher terrain. Temperatures will climb a degree or two by \nTuesday as well, prompting the expansion of the Heat Advisory \nnorthwards up the Rio Grande into El Paso. \n\nThe upper ridge will begin to drift west again on Wednesday, with\nnortherly flow perhaps sparking a few storms over the Sacramentos.\nLow level east flow Wednesday night will boost low level moisture,\nand weak disturbances aloft dropping in from the north will help\nincrease thunderstorm coverage across a wider area on Thursday \nand Friday.\n\nThe ridge will again refocus to our east towards the weekend, as \na trough digs into the Pacific Northwest, breaking down the\nwestern edge of the ridge. This will pull a plume of monsoonal\nmoisture up from the south, favoring thunderstorm coverage, but\nmostly west of the Rio Grande.",
